  "slug": "architecture-naturalistic-information-e2c5",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link state-dependent decision and communication dynamics to distributed prefrontal, parietal and striatal circuits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that decision and attention variables are carried by state-dependent network interactions rather than fixed regional responses. In a human fMRI bandit task, working-memory load reduced directed exploration and reconfigured aIns-IPS coupling. In rhesus monkeys, prefrontal neurons in ACC, OFC and DLPFC encoded and updated value according to the resource state of an earning-spending task. Graphical Ising models fitted to OFC spike data indicate that offer values shift network attractors toward relative-value choice states. Communication subspaces spanning prefrontal cortex, anterior cingulate and caudate emerge before gaze-directed object selection and develop with learning. What is new across these preprints is the joint use of state-dependent measures and reduced network descriptions, applied to exploration, valuation, choice and attentional selection across human and primate recordings.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
